Serra had always been a promising student of the "Kenjitsu no Kaze"
dojo.  One day, while meditating, the goddess Amaterasu Omikami came to
her in a vision. The goddess revealed that Serra had a great destiny.
After speaking with her master, Kotetsu Enshiro, and her Daimyo, Lord
Sato, she set off on her quest. She traveled Japan from one end to the
other, in search of the entrance to hell.  Eventually she found a
mysterious staicase leading deep into the earth.

On the first level of what appeared to be a massive, convoluted, and
dangerous structure, Serra found some useful stuff lying around.  A
ring (ooh, shiny!), a lamp, and a scroll.  "Some mage must have lost
these, perhaps I'll run into him ..." A short while later, she found an
altar to the sun goddess Amaterasu Omikami. Remembering something her
master told her, she dropped all her gear on the altar.  The lamp was
blessed!

A dirty blessed lamp is a very_bad_thing(tm), and so Serra polished it
up a bit.  While cleaning her lamp, she freed a djinni, who, greatful
for his release, asked "For what do you wish?"

A dilemma.  Serra had heard stories of magical armour and weapons of
great power.  She had also remembered watching a wandering ronin
practice with a very sharp and very stainless two-handed sword.  It was
a simple case of tsurugi envy.

{ Weapon Comparison:EnBON	SkBON	ToHit	SDAM	SAVG	LDAM	LAVG
katana & wakizashi	+0/+0	-9/-3	+1	d10+d6	 6.0	d12+d8	 8.0
+3 tsurugi		+3/+3	-4/-2	+2	d16	 9.5	d8+2d6	12.5
}

She couldn't let this whim a decide for her, so she read several
"scrolls of spoiling" and meditated on her answer for several hours.

Finally, the djinni folded his arms and scowled "I'm waiting."

Startled from her meditations she blurted "a blessed +3 tsurugi"

"It's about damn time!" the djinni said, before vanishing.

A while later, on what line of status said was the fourth level of the
dungeon, she came across an unusual sight.  The entire level consisted
of a large, unlit, courtyard with several rooms.  This is not good, she
thought.  Serra was well aware that large, open spaces often lead to
incurable cases of death.

There was a dwarf corpse with a ghost named Swaney hovering nearby.  He
must not have known that open space was a very_bad_thing(tm).
Especially when there is a trigger happy elf (with a wand of lightning)
wandering about.  It didn't help that the elf had some elven friends.

Fortunately for Serra the wand of lightning only had one charge.  After
a nice game of OH-CRAP-I-NEED-TO-PRAY-LIKE-RIGHT-NOW!  Serra felt much
better. This was followed up with a rather fun and relaxing game of hit
the elves until they die.  The winners prize was a very nice mithril
suit, complete with all the elven accoutrements (shoes, helmet, cloak).
 Now all she needed were some gloves, and "oh look, there's a pair in
Swaney's sack."

About this time, Serra noticed that it had been a very long time since
she had been hungry.  She figured the uncursed shiny ring had something
to do with this.  After reading some cheap scrolls she bought off Ms.
Upernavik, she learned that the ring meant she could continue following
her very strict diet.

Three downstairs later, she said ... "Goody, another pair of shoes!"
(Serra has a weakness for footwear).  She tried them on, and after an
experimental #jump, she discovered just what she had.

A short while later she found a closet with some writing outside it and
oh crap that's a trap door.  Serra must have landed inside some creepy
demon's bedroom, because an incubus tried to seduce her.  She told the
incubus,  "Nobody gets inside *MY* armour, so stop that."  He wouldn't
stop, so she had to hit him with her tsurugi.

Another trapdoor, and "You hear convulsive ravings."  I wonder what
that's about, Serra thought, before exploring.  A sign said "Welcome to
Delphi" and someone named the Oracle asked her whether she wanted a big
or little consultation.  She bought both.  The big one was something
about Medusa and mirrors, and apparently the wizard has hired some
help.

Because she hadn't fully mapped out the 7th and 8th floors, Serra
decided to head upwards and "You enter an opulent throne room."  It was
filled with sleeping nasties, of course.  They didn't stay sleeping for
very long.  The creepy demon who wouldn't take "NO!" for an answer
KABOOM!ed the doorway.

After cleaning out the throne room, and finally offing the creepy
incubus, Serra needed a bit of a rest.  She sat on the throne, got two
wishes and a "You feel smart!"  The first she used on some blessed gray
dragon scale mail and the second on some scrolls.  After reading her
scrolls, she knew she wouldn't have to deal with either *trices or
*liches.

A short while later, she played a relaxing boulder moving game a got a
nifty bag as the grand prize.  By now she had collected a whole bunch
of wands, and decided to identify them:

 r) wishing (0:1) <-- sweet!

Wishfest:
	(0:1)	2 blessed scrolls of charging (got 1)
	(1:3)	blessed fireproof +2 gauntlets of dexterity
	(1:2)	blessed greased rustproof +2 helm of brilliance
	(1:1)	blessed fireproof +2 robe
	(1:0)	blessed spellbook of identify

That must easily be some sort of record, Serra thought, 8 wishes in the
first ten levels.  She mediatated for a bit while deciding what to do
next.  She knew that if she went up to the mines, she would just end up
murdering a bunch of gnomes, and sounded more boring than fun, so she
decided to go downwards and take care of that Medusa person.

Another downstairs (Lv.11) and another throne room.  This time she
didn't get any wishes, but was shocked for her trouble before
genociding "master mind flayers."

On her way to go deal with Medusa, she got a telepathic message from
her daimyo, Lord Sato, while in this very strange and older part of the
dungeon.  Serra went to go see what that was about.  Apparrently
Ashikaga Takauji had stolen the Tsurugi of Muramasa.  Sato-sama told
her to go find some more teachers before undertaking this quest Serra
was just a lowly Joshu).

Further down, she entered a shrine to Amaterasu Omikami and chatted
with the priestess there for awhile.  Upon leaving, she had the feeling
that her devotion had been rewarded and that she was well protected.

